## Executive summary

1. The loudest instrument in an MWAA hold room during a slot-constrained departure is not the airport public-address system. It is a gate agent's handheld on speaker, layered over concessionaire audio and tenant televisions. Under the 2025 Use and Lease Agreement, the airport owns the room; the airline, the tenant, and the ground handler own most of what makes it sound the way it sounds.[^1]

2. A "quiet-by-design" standard written only into the base-terminal volume of the Airports Authority Design Manual will govern less than half of the noise floor. The lever that matters is the tenant design volumes, the airline lease exhibits, and the commissioning protocol — the instruments through which MWAA already regulates concession fitouts, gate hold-room finishes, and substantial-completion acceptance.[^2]

3. The intelligibility floor is not a preference; it is code. NFPA 72 requires a Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, and an average of 0.50, verified under IEC 60268-16 STIPA methodology.[^3] ACRP Research Report 175 lifts the working target to 0.50 with a signal-to-noise margin of at least 10 dB(A) in pier hold rooms.[^4] Every "quiet" claim rides on top of these numbers.

4. Redundant channels are federal law. Under 14 CFR Part 382, flight-critical information — boarding, gate assignment, paging, delays, cancellations, emergencies — must be accessible; televisions capable of captioning must caption at all times.[^5] Under 28 CFR §35.160, MWAA as a Title II entity must give "primary consideration" to the passenger's requested aid.[^6] Chennai's 2017 silent-terminal rollout is the operational failure mode this regulatory floor was written to prevent.[^7]

5. DCA needs a higher announcement floor than IAD, not a lower one. DCA is capped at 67 hourly slots and operates on a single main runway; a boarding missed because a passenger was in a quiet zone is a chargeable, unrecoverable event.[^8] Under the 2025 Use and Lease, MWAA receives $1 million per new beyond-perimeter flight pair from DCA net remaining revenue, conditioned on DCA maintaining a $2 CPE advantage over IAD.[^9] Any DCA acoustic premium that closes that gap has contracted revenue consequences.

6. The timing is binding, not aspirational. Concourse E — 435,000 square feet, 14 United gates, 46,000 square feet of concessions, direct AeroTrain access — opens later in 2026.[^10] The July 2026 joint MWAA–United–USDOT announcement puts more than 5 million square feet of IAD terminal space into design over the coming decade at an order-of-magnitude of $20 billion to $22.5 billion.[^11] Acoustic ceiling treatment costs $5 to $15 per square foot in new construction and $18 to $45 per square foot as retrofit.[^12] Every square foot that ships without acoustic, PA-zoning, and information-system specifications is a square foot the Authority will pay two to four times as much to fix.

7. The affordability envelope is spending headroom, not carrying it. MWAA's 2025A Official Statement forecasts signatory cost per enplanement at IAD rising from $12.77 today to $20.71 by 2030 under the existing $9 billion Capital Construction Program, before the incremental Dulles vision is priced in; adjusted debt per O&D enplanement is projected to peak near $400 in 2028.[^13] Written now, the entire quiet-by-design capital stack is on the order of $12 million to $40 million — 0.06 to 0.20 percent of the announced IAD program, and $0.03 to $0.11 per enplanement.[^14] Written after design freezes, it is a change-order fight against a rising CPE curve.

8. Adopt the standard as an amendment to the Airports Authority Design Manual, name it the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard, and route it as a Design Manual amendment rather than a discrete capital program. Never use the word "quiet" in the resolution.

