## The hold room MWAA does not own

Start at Gate 35 of DCA Terminal 2 on a weekday morning. The 1997 Pelli National Hall opens into a long, hard-surfaced concourse. American Airlines, which holds roughly a 53 percent share at DCA, is turning slot-capped narrowbodies against a 67-slot-per-hour cap.[^15] A gate agent picks up a handheld and calls Group 4 in a voice that competes with a muted-but-audible television at the concession across the way, a rolling wheel of announcements from a neighboring gate whose zone the agent cannot silence, and the HVAC that runs the length of the pier. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back leans in. A passenger who does not carry a smartphone watches for the visual cue that is not there. The boarding pass in her hand says Group 3.

MWAA owns the room. It does not own the sound.

That sentence is the operative fact of a quiet-by-design standard at MWAA. The Authority controls the shell — the ceiling, the finishes, the base-building PA — and specifies it through Volume 1 and Volume 2 of the Airports Authority Design Manual. It controls tenant fitouts, gate hold-room standards, and concession finish palettes through Volumes 2 and 3.[^2] It controls emergency notification through NFPA 72 compliance in the fire-alarm envelope. It does not control the airline gate microphone; the 2025 Use and Lease grants signatory carriers broadcast rights inside their leased envelope, and that grant is fifteen years long.[^1] It does not control the tenant television, which lives inside the concessionaire's lease. And it does not control the ground handler's handheld, which lives inside another contract entirely. The single loudest thing in a US-hub hold room is almost always something MWAA neither installs nor operates.

That is not a reason to abandon the standard. It is the reason to write it where the microphones actually live.

## What the peers actually did

Every airport the trade press calls "silent" governs exceptions. London City Airport's silent-terminal policy, in effect since 2008, expressly retains announcements for short-notice gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure.[^16] Helsinki-Vantaa, silent since 1 June 2015, retains gate and emergency announcements and had to add banners under its flight information displays telling passengers they now had to track time themselves.[^17] Amsterdam Schiphol describes itself as running "virtually no announcements" and pairs that policy with a formal accessibility program for deaf and blind passengers.[^18] Copenhagen zones quiet by gate area rather than airport-wide.[^19] San Francisco — the only documented U.S. large-hub program — reports a roughly 40 percent reduction in boarding-area announcements and about 90 minutes per day removed from the International Terminal; passenger complaints about missed information are also on the public record.[^20] Chennai's 2017 rollout produced a disability-community backlash serious enough that airport officials publicly conceded "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged are available."[^7]

Two conclusions follow. The first is that no peer operator has published measured missed-boarding, missed-connection, or irregular-operations data attributable to a silent-airport policy. Any argument built on peer branding is design conservatism, not measured outcome, and should be defended as such. The second is that every one of these programs is an exception-governance regime, not the absence of a PA. The interesting variable at MWAA is not how much silence to inherit; it is what the exceptions are, who authorizes them, and in which zone.

## The floors the standard inherits

Before MWAA writes anything, it inherits three regulatory floors. NFPA 72, adopted through the Virginia Statewide Fire Prevention Code and the D.C. Construction Codes, requires an STI of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space and an average of 0.50, verified under IEC 60268-16 STIPA methodology at commissioning.[^3] The Fairfax County (IAD) and Arlington County (DCA) fire marshals enforce this at certificate of occupancy. ACRP Research Report 175 — the airport-industry design document Wilson Ihrig authored for TRB in 2017 — targets STI 0.50 for terminal PA and at least 10 dB(A) of announcement-to-ambient margin in pier hold rooms.[^4] And 14 CFR Part 382 requires accessible delivery of every flight-critical message the terminal produces, with televisions capable of captioning captioning at all times.[^5] 28 CFR §35.160 extends the same "as effective" standard to MWAA as a Title II entity.[^6]

Reduce announcements without maintaining these floors and the standard fails plan review — first at the fire marshal, then at the Department of Transportation, and then, eventually, in a Part 382 complaint filed by a passenger the standard was said to protect.

## Two airports, two calibrations

DCA and IAD are not the same operation, and the standard should not pretend they are. DCA closed 2025 at 24.9 million passengers, down 5.3 percent year over year and its lowest total since 2022, absorbing the January 2025 midair collision, a seven-week fall federal shutdown, and softer federal-workforce travel demand.[^21] It runs on a single main runway, capped at 67 hourly slots, with a heavy federal-official and business mix and a short curb-to-gate journey.[^15] Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten beyond-perimeter exemptions, awarded in December 2024 with service required to begin by March 2025.[^8] A denied boarding costs an airline between $2,300 and $16,000; a narrowbody delay minute costs roughly $100.[^22] At DCA those numbers move higher because a missed slot cannot be re-flown. The mandatory announcement floor at DCA is therefore higher than at IAD, not lower — the opposite of what a "quieter is better" reading of the peer literature would predict.

IAD ran roughly 29 million passengers in 2025, including 10.5 million international — both all-time highs.[^23] Its curb-to-gate journeys are longer, its passenger populations more multilingual, and its transfer flows more dependent on visual and digital redundancy than on English audio. The standard's calibration at IAD is density and redundancy of the visual and digital channels; the calibration at DCA is intelligibility and reliability of the audible one.

One standard. Two calibrations. Written as one common intelligibility floor with an explicit DCA variance for hold-room announcement density and IROPS PA envelope.

## The counter-case, honestly

The strongest argument against writing a new standard is that MWAA does not need one. NFPA 72, ADA Title II, ACAA, and a competent commissioning specification already require what a quiet-by-design standard would require; ACRP 175 already exists as a project-level reference; a paragraph in each consultant's scope naming ACRP 175 and requiring STIPA verification in occupied conditions would deliver most of the value without a board resolution. The Board has not asked for this; the White House and USDOT have made the Dulles program politically visible; and the January 2025 DCA midair is still in memory. A "quiet" framing at MWAA in 2026 will be read against that memory. The Contrarian is right on all of that.

The concession the standard must therefore make is threefold. It should not be sold as a new requirement; it should be sold as the integration layer across requirements that today live in different consultants' scopes on different projects with no owner for the seam. It should not carry the word "quiet" in any external MWAA document; it should carry the name Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard, for exactly the reason Schiphol and Helsinki brand silence externally but govern intelligibility internally. And it should say nothing about airline announcement scripting inside the leased envelope — that lives in the Use and Lease, is contested through majority-in-interest, and is a fight MWAA does not need to open before 2039.

What the standard should say — and what the Contrarian's position cannot deliver project by project — is what the base building must achieve, what the tenant volumes must achieve on top of it, what the airline lease exhibits require by way of gate-PA discipline for slot-constrained departures, and what commissioning must verify in occupied conditions before substantial completion. That is the integration layer. Berlin Brandenburg is the cautionary tale for what happens when it is missing: a bespoke smoke-extraction and voice-notification integration that never certified against a frozen design, an initial €2 billion budget that closed at €7.3 billion, and a nine-year schedule slip.[^24] Denver's automated baggage system is the same story in a different subsystem: contract escalation from $193 million to over $400 million, a 16-month opening delay at roughly $1 million per day of carrying cost, and abandonment in August 2005.[^25] Both were technology aspirations colliding with frozen architectural envelopes. Both were governance failures written into acoustics-adjacent specifications. Both were preventable at design.

## Where it gets written, and by when

Adopt the standard as an amendment to the Airports Authority Design Manual, scoped inside the FY26 revision cycle, and structured in three tiers. The mandatory floor applies to both airports: NFPA 72 STI verified under IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions; ACRP 175 signal-to-noise margin in every pier hold room; a redundant-channel obligation naming audible, visual/digital, and staffed delivery for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; PA-push authority anchored to a named position with pre-authorized override; and an emergency-notification stack specified on generator plus UPS plus local battery, with a duty-officer analog fallback for the day the managed-IT layer goes down as it did across major hubs on 19 July 2024.[^26] Recommended practice covers NC/RC targets by space type, quiet-zone locations, and employee sound-exposure monitoring under OSHA 1910.95.[^27] Context-dependent options cover the DCA variance clause and preservation carve-outs for the Saarinen Main Terminal at IAD and the 1941 Terminal 1 at DCA.

Route it as a Design Manual amendment inside existing consultant fees. General counsel should confirm before the Board memo is drafted whether the amendment triggers the majority-in-interest thresholds in the 2025 Use and Lease — those thresholds are not fully public, and the answer determines whether this is a board resolution or an airline negotiation. If the answer is board resolution, the executive owner is the President and CEO with the Chief Operating Officer and Vice President of Design and Construction as co-sponsors, and the first 90-day action is instructing the Design Manual working group to insert acoustic and information performance criteria into Volume 1, the IAD tenant volumes, and the DCA parallel volumes before the Concourse C and D program brief is issued. The leading indicator is Concourse E STIPA data at commissioning and again 60 days after opening; the failure mode to guard against is scope migration from an acoustic-and-information envelope into an announcement-policy standard the airlines contest under the Use and Lease. The standard stops proceeding if the MII review indicates it becomes an incremental capital line item; in that case it is renegotiated as a side-letter to the next Use and Lease review, not adopted as an aspirational document.

## A last image

Return to the hold room at DCA Terminal 2. Under the standard, the ceiling is different, the wall absorption is different, and the base-building loudspeakers are laid out to reach every seat with an STI the fire marshal will accept in occupied conditions. The airline gate PA is unchanged; American still calls Group 3. The tenant television is captioned and muted, per federal rule and per the amended tenant handbook. The FIDS updates within an SLA that survives a gate change inside T-25 minutes. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back hears the boarding call cleanly. A passenger without a smartphone reads the same information off a display. The gate agent, no longer competing with adjacent-zone spillover, spends the next four minutes with the family that missed the boarding sequence rather than with the microphone.

Nothing in that room is quiet in the marketing sense. Every message that matters lands. That is the standard MWAA can write, and it is the only one worth writing before the concrete cures.

